{"ts": "2026-08-17T10:55:50Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 114, "kind": "message", "text": "I've mapped the codebase \u2014 this is a small in-memory task tracker with a FastAPI backend (`backend/app/{models,store,main}.py`) and a React frontend (`frontend/src/{App.jsx,api.js,components/*}`). Two prior features (filter-by-done, edit-title) were each shipped in a single cycle touching the same full stack, so I'm triaging this as a **Feature** \u2014 comparable in size \u2014 not a multi-feature story, despite the \"story\" stage label on the dispatch.\n\nOne thing before I write: `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md` currently hold the previous cycle's content (edit-a-task-title, already merged per `git log`). I'd overwrite both. Confirm that's fine, or say if you want them preserved elsewhere first.\n\nHere's the finished spec I'd write to `docs/spec.md`:\n\n---\n\n## Goal\nLet a task carry a low/normal/high priority, show that priority at a glance in the list, and have high-priority tasks float to the top of whatever's currently displayed \u2014 without reordering anything else.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Creating a task without a priority defaults it to `normal`.\n2. Creating a task with an explicit priority (`low`/`normal`/`high`) persists that value.\n3. Changing an existing task's priority updates only priority \u2014 id, title, done stay unchanged.\n4. A priority outside `{low, normal, high}` is rejected with `422` on both create and update; no task is mutated.\n5. Given list order `[A:normal, B:high, C:low, D:high, E:normal]`, the displayed order is `[B, D, A, C, E]` \u2014 all `high` tasks first, in their original relative order, then everyone else (normal and low, interleaved) in their original relative order.\n6. If no task is `high`, display order is unchanged from today (id order).\n7. The Open/Done filter still works exactly as now; priority sort applies **within** the filtered subset \u2014 a high-priority Done task doesn't jump into the Open view.\n8. A task's priority is visible without opening its edit form (e.g. a badge on the row).\n9. Moving a task's priority away from `high` moves it out of the top group on the next render.\n10. Editing a task's title via the existing `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}` flow leaves its priority untouched (same precedent as it already leaving `done` untouched).\n\n## Non-goals\n- No new filter/sort control for priority \u2014 the sort is automatic, not a UI toggle.\n- No priority levels beyond low/normal/high.\n- No bulk priority editing.\n- No change to `done` filtering/sorting behavior.\n- No priority history/audit trail.\n\n## Affected areas\n**Backend**\n- `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 add a `priority` field to `Task` (default `\"normal\"`); extend `TaskIn` or add a dedicated input model for priority changes.\n- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 `TaskStore.add()` takes priority; add `TaskStore.set_priority()` alongside the existing `set_done()`.\n- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 `create_task` passes priority through; add `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/priority` (mirrors the existing `/done` action-scoped route rather than overloading the title-only `PATCH /{id}`).\n- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 tests for default priority, explicit priority on create, priority update, 422 on invalid value, and title-update leaving priority untouched.\n\n**Frontend**\n- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 add `updatePriority(id, priority)`; extend `createTask` to send priority.\n- `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx` \u2014 priority selector at creation, default `normal`.\n- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 priority badge per row, plus a control to change priority without entering title-edit mode.\n- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 add a stable sort step (high-priority first) after the existing `visibleTasks` filter (`App.jsx:27-31`).\n- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 styling for the priority badge/control.\n\n## Edge cases\n- Multiple `high` tasks keep their relative order among themselves.\n- All-normal/no-`high` list renders identically to today.\n- Priority is independent of `done` in both directions.\n- Invalid priority string on create or update \u2192 `422`, no partial mutation.\n- Missing priority on create \u2192 defaults to `normal`.\n- `404` on priority-update of a nonexistent task id (mirrors `/done` and title-`PATCH` 404s).\n\n## Open questions (proceeding under these assumptions)\n1. **Does `low` sort below `normal`, or stay interleaved?** Assuming interleaved \u2014 only `high` moves, per the literal ask (\"without disturbing the order of everything else\"). A full 3-tier sort is a plausible alternate reading; flagging since it's cheap to change now, costly after ux/dev build against the wrong one.\n2. **Settable at creation, or only after?** Assuming both \u2014 `AddTask` gets a selector (default `normal`), and existing rows get a way to change it that doesn't require opening the title-edit form.\n3. **HTTP contract for changing priority.** Assuming a new `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/priority` endpoint, matching the existing `/done` precedent.\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you want me to adjust anything (especially open question #1 \u2014 interleaved vs. full 3-tier sort), and confirm the docs overwrite, and I'll write `docs/spec.md` and hand off to the ux-designer stage.", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "5050ad49-a79b-4044-8c08-7ab6658e49c6"}}
